{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates lotteries based on the sure outcome (the one with minimum absolute value) and then considers the differences between that sure outcome and the other outcomes, weighted by their probabilities. In this problem, Lottery A has a sure outcome of 1500 Euros (100% probability), which has an absolute value of 1500. Lottery B has two outcomes: +3000 and -3000, each with 50% probability. The absolute values are 3000 and 3000. The minimum absolute value is 1500 (from A), so A is the sure outcome. The differences from this sure outcome are: for B, the difference from 1500 to 3000 is +1500 (with 50% prob), and to -3000 is -4500 (with 50% prob). The weighted sum of differences would be: 0.5*(+1500) + 0.5*(-4500) = 750 - 2250 = -1500. The sum of the sure outcome (1500) and the weighted differences is 1500 + (-1500) = 0. For A, the sure outcome is 1500, and there are no differences (only one outcome), so the sum is 1500. Since 1500 > 0, the reason prefers A. Thus, the decision reason is applicable and can be applied to this decision problem.",
